## Local implementation status

This is a local implementation, not a published or production-ready release.
Deployment, Service, ServiceAccount, Ingress, CronJobs, the readiness hook,
namespaced sandbox RBAC, portable/Cilium policies and ServiceMonitor are implemented.
Release delivery and live cluster acceptance remain gated. Create the sandbox
namespace beforehand, separate from the app namespace. RBAC grants pods
create/get/list/delete and pods/exec get/create only. Policies select provider
labels managed-by=chamvi and component=sandbox, never app pods. Portable policy
denies ingress and allows selected DNS plus public HTTPS, excluding configured
cluster CIDRs and metadata. Configure all cluster/service/node CIDRs for your
network and use an enforcing CNI. Cilium replaces, never unions with, portable
policy, requires its API and restricts HTTPS to configured DNS names/patterns.
ServiceMonitor is skipped without its API. Live enforcement remains unverified.

The official generators are gated in this environment. This is the helm-docs
source template, not a generated README. `values.schema.json` is also pending;
runtime guards and local tests do not replace schema generation or its drift gate.

## Install

After publication and acceptance, the intended public package is
`oci://ghcr.io/chamvi/helm/agent-app`. Until then render the local source:

```sh
helm template triage ./charts/agent-app -n agents -f values.yaml
```

When the package is available:

```sh
helm install triage oci://ghcr.io/chamvi/helm/agent-app --version 0.1.0 -n agents -f values.yaml
```

Minimal application values (the image must contain the built app and CLI):

```yaml
image:
    repository: ghcr.io/example/triage
    tag: main-abc1234-1792540800
secrets: [triage-env]
```

A published package will be consumable through Flux with no registry credential
for the public chart. The agent image may have its own access requirements.

```yaml
apiVersion: source.toolkit.fluxcd.io/v1
kind: OCIRepository
metadata:
    name: triage-chart
    namespace: flux-system
spec:
    interval: 1h
    url: oci://ghcr.io/chamvi/helm/agent-app
    ref:
        semver: '>=0.1.0 <0.2.0'
    layerSelector:
        mediaType: application/vnd.cncf.helm.chart.content.v1.tar+gzip
        operation: copy
---
apiVersion: helm.toolkit.fluxcd.io/v2
kind: HelmRelease
metadata:
    name: triage
    namespace: agents
spec:
    interval: 10m
    chartRef:
        kind: OCIRepository
        name: triage-chart
        namespace: flux-system
    values:
        image:
            repository: ghcr.io/example/triage # {"$imagepolicy": "flux-system:triage:name"}
            tag: main-abc1234-1792540800 # {"$imagepolicy": "flux-system:triage:tag"}
        secrets: [triage-env]
        basePath: /agents
        ingress:
            enabled: true
            host: agents.example.com
            tls:
                secretName: agents-tls
```

## Application and credentials

Keep the explicit `src/index.ts` application entry as the normal app layout.
The image must contain the `chamvi build` output and production dependencies.
The chart appends `start` to `command` as container **args**, never overrides
`command`, and therefore preserves the image's tini entrypoint. Optional
`CHAMVI_AGENTS_DIR` agent folders may be supplied by a custom image or mount;
this chart does not distribute agent code or create an agents volume.

`secrets` names existing Secrets consumed through `envFrom` on the Deployment
and Jobs. This chart creates no Secret. Use SOPS or External Secrets to manage
`DATABASE_URL`, `CHAMVI_TOKENS`, provider API keys, `GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY`,
`GITHUB_WEBHOOK_SECRET` and other credentials. `env` is for non-sensitive scalar
values. Helm cannot inspect Secret contents, prevent collisions hidden inside
`envFrom`, or check the effective shutdown timeout there. Explicit chart env
wins over a same-named variable in `envFrom`.

`basePath` prefixes health checks, readiness and the Ingress route. The derived
`CHAMVI_PUBLIC_URL` is only `https://<host>` (with `tls.secretName`) or
`http://<host>` (without it). The app adds `basePath` once when constructing UI
approval links. Set `env.CHAMVI_PUBLIC_URL` explicitly if TLS terminates outside
the Ingress; it is emitted once and overrides derivation. GitHub intake is
`<basePath>/channels/github`. Approval links carry IDs, never credentials.

## Shutdown and security

The defaults are a native 5-second preStop sleep, a 90-second CLI force-exit
timeout, and a 120-second pod grace period. Startup probes allow 120 seconds
for initialization; liveness and readiness use the same configured HTTP prefix.
The chart fails when `preStopSleepSeconds * 1000 + CHAMVI_FORCE_EXIT_MS` is not
strictly less than `terminationGracePeriodSeconds * 1000`. Put the timeout in
`env` if you need the chart to validate it. A Secret's timeout is invisible to
Helm. A zero preStop omits the lifecycle hook.

Pods run as UID/GID 1000, with a read-only root filesystem, no privilege
escalation, all capabilities dropped, RuntimeDefault seccomp and a writable
`/tmp` emptyDir. The ServiceAccount defaults to no auto-mounted token. The app
pod requests a token only for sandbox integration; the readiness hook never
receives application env, Secrets or a service account token. Private image
pull settings still reach all pods. The hook uses the image's Node runtime,
not a shell or curl, with a bounded readiness request.

## CronJob mode

```yaml
cronJobs:
    - name: digest
      schedule: '0 7 * * *'
      timeZone: Europe/Berlin
      agent: digest
      subscription: 'cron:0 7 * * *'
```

These must match the agent's `cron.on()` subscriptions from
`@chamvi/channel-cron`. `subscription` remains one argv element, spaces included;
it may be omitted for an agent with exactly one cron subscription. IDs starting
with a dash are rejected because the CLI interprets them as options.

Any entry disables the in-process scheduler for the whole Deployment, so mirror
every cron subscription. Supply a shared PostgreSQL `DATABASE_URL` via Secret:
MemoryStore cannot share approvals, runs or locks across Jobs and the Deployment.
Jobs use `Forbid`, `restartPolicy: Never` and `backoffLimit: 0`; a retry would
create a new scheduled-at idempotency key and could repeat work. The chart does
not retry a failed Job. `cronJob.resources` overrides Deployment resources;
an empty map inherits them. Jobs share security and scheduling settings, but
have no HTTP probes, ports or preStop delay.

## Verification and generation

`make test` runs local Helm lint, helm-unittest, the real-render Python contract
suite, the render matrix and literal values coverage. Requires Helm 4.2.4 or
compatible, helm-unittest 1.2.1, Python 3 with PyYAML, and yamllint. The optional
`CHAMVI_APP_MODULE` environment variable points to a built `@chamvi/app` entry
for a real approval URL integration check; otherwise that check is explicitly
skipped. Using the monorepo build is not registry-install verification.

The approved tool pins are helm-docs v1.14.2, helm-schema 0.18.1 and
chart-testing v3.15.0. `make docs`, `make schema` and `make ct-lint` currently
fail explicitly pending tool authorization. No custom schema or documentation
generator is supplied. `make generated-check` also fails until both official
outputs exist. After authorization, restore the upstream tool invocations,
generate both outputs, check drift and rerun the suites against the schema.
CI values are render fixtures only: they refer to an unavailable local fixture
image and externally provided Secret, not an install-verified application.

